<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uppsala</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uppsala is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>. Nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamla-uppsala">Old Uppsala</a>, with its royal burial mounds, was a centre of the pre-Christian kings of the Swedes and of a pagan temple described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a>. Uppsala became the seat of Sweden's archbishops in 1164, and its university, founded in 1477, is the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. Its university library holds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-argenteus">Codex Argenteus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uppsala.jpg" alt="Uppsala" width="320" height="569" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Axebackman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uppsala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Svear of the Mälaren valley and Uppland, centred on the royal and cult site of <b>Uppsala</b>, appear in sources from Tacitus onward, while the Geats lived in Västergötland and Östergötland to the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…as history in Latin, including the story of Amleth, the original of Shakespeare’s Hamlet; the German chronicler Adam of Bremen described the temple at <b>Uppsala</b> around 1075; and the Arab envoy Ibn Fadlan described the ship funeral of a chieftain of the Rus on the Volga in 922.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The finest is the Codex Argenteus, now in <b>Uppsala</b>, 188 leaves holding a large part of the four gospels; its language is full of Greek words and usages, and its syntax often copies the Greek directly.</blockquote><a href="/language/gothic">Gothic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Adam of Bremen, writing about 1075, described a temple at <b>Uppsala</b> with images of Thor, Odin and Freyr.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>In Sweden, the last stronghold of the Norse gods, Adam of Bremen described the temple at <b>Uppsala</b>, with its images of Thor, Odin and Freyr, around 1075; by the 12th century Scandinavia was Christian.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>It survives in a single source, the Cancionero de Upsala, printed in Venice in 1556, where it is the fortieth song; the one known copy is kept in the library of the University of <b>Uppsala</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/riu-riu-chiu">Ríu Ríu Chíu</a></li></ul></section>
